    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_pipepager`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_pipepager` function is a utility within the Click library that pipes the output of a generator to an external command, typically a pager like `less`, for display. It exists to handle the display of potentially large amounts of text in a user-friendly manner, allowing the user to scroll through the output. The function takes three parameters: `generator`, an iterable of strings to be displayed; `cmd`, the command to which the output will be piped; and `color`, a boolean or `None` indicating whether to preserve ANSI color codes in the output. The function returns nothing (`None`). It relies on the `subprocess` module to execute the command and handle the input/output streams, and on the `get_best_encoding` and `strip_ansi` helper functions to manage text encoding and color stripping, respectively. The function handles `OSError` and `KeyboardInterrupt` exceptions gracefully, ensuring that the subprocess is terminated properly even if the user interrupts the process. It also includes a loop to wait for the subprocess to complete, handling additional `KeyboardInterrupt` exceptions to ensure that the pager exits cleanly if the user interrupts it. The side effects include executing an external command, potentially modifying the environment, and altering the terminal state.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
